# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Azure AI Foundry "work profile" — example .env
#
# Copy the values you need into your repo `.env` OR (preferred for shared
# secrets) into `~/.audit-loop.env` (chmod 600, never committed). The Azure
# path is OPT-IN: it activates only when AZURE_OPENAI_ENDPOINT is set. With
# these vars absent, the bundle uses the public OpenAI/Gemini/Anthropic APIs
# exactly as before (byte-identical).
#
# See docs/runbooks/azure-work-profile.md for the full deployment guide.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# ── Azure AI Foundry ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Your Azure AI Foundry / Azure OpenAI key (portal → Keys and Endpoint).
AZURE_OPENAI_API_KEY=

# Azure OpenAI endpoint — serves GPT (auditor) + embeddings. The resource root;
# do NOT append /openai or /openai/v1. A trailing slash is fine (it is stripped).
# If you front Azure OpenAI with an API gateway, include the gateway's base path
# here (e.g. https://<your-gateway>/<your-route>) — the path is preserved and the
# operation route is appended after it.
AZURE_OPENAI_ENDPOINT=https://<your-resource>.openai.azure.com

# Azure AI Foundry endpoint — the DIRECT Foundry host. Only needed on the
# `foundry` Claude route (below); leave it unset if Claude is served through your
# API Management gateway.
AZURE_AI_ENDPOINT=https://<your-resource>.services.ai.azure.com

# ── Which Claude route? ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Claude reaches your tenant one of two ways, and they are DIFFERENT SERVICES —
# different host, different credential, different auth header:
#
#   apim     <AZURE_OPENAI_ENDPOINT>/anthropic   api-key header   AZURE_OPENAI_API_KEY
#   foundry  <AZURE_AI_ENDPOINT>/anthropic       Bearer           AZURE_AI_API_KEY (else AZURE_OPENAI_API_KEY)
#
# Unset → `foundry` when AZURE_AI_ENDPOINT is set, else `apim`.
#
# SET THIS TO `apim` IF YOUR TENANT FRONTS FOUNDRY WITH API MANAGEMENT, even
# though AZURE_AI_ENDPOINT is also set: the direct Foundry host will not accept
# an APIM subscription key, and the APIM host rejects Bearer with "Access denied
# due to missing subscription key".
# AZURE_CLAUDE_ROUTE=apim

# Dedicated key for the DIRECT Foundry host. Set this when the `foundry` route's
# key differs from AZURE_OPENAI_API_KEY (it usually does behind a gateway).
# Without it the foundry route falls back to AZURE_OPENAI_API_KEY — permitted,
# but reported as [SHARED across services] because that key is then being sent to
# a service it may not belong to.
# AZURE_AI_API_KEY=

# Verify all of the above without printing a single secret:
#   npm run azure:routes

# api-version. Default: 2025-03-01-preview, the DEPLOYMENT-QUALIFIED Azure OpenAI
# surface used for GPT + embeddings:
#   <endpoint>/openai/deployments/<deployment>/chat/completions
#   <endpoint>/openai/deployments/<deployment>/embeddings
#   <endpoint>/openai/responses            (not deployment-qualified, by design)
# Set this only to pin a different dated version your resource requires. Note the
# Foundry/Claude route keeps the undated `preview` sentinel of the /openai/v1
# surface unless you override it here (this var applies to both).
# AZURE_OPENAI_API_VERSION=2025-03-01-preview

# ── Final-review provider (REQUIRED on the work repo) ─────────────────────────
# The default reviewer is Gemini whenever GEMINI_API_KEY is present — a
# configured Azure profile no longer auto-replaces it. To make THIS repo use
# Azure Foundry Opus for the final review permanently, set this (or run
# `node scripts/gemini-review.mjs set-provider azure-claude`). It wins even if a
# Gemini key leaks in via ~/.audit-loop.env. Values: gemini | azure-claude | anthropic.
FINAL_REVIEW_PROVIDER=azure-claude

# ── Deployment names (Azure *deployment* names) ───────────────────────────────
# GPT auditor: you can set AZURE_OPENAI_GPT_DEPLOYMENT, OR just put the
# deployment name in OPENAI_AUDIT_MODEL below (the natural way) — a concrete
# (non-sentinel) OPENAI_AUDIT_MODEL is used as the deployment automatically.
# This is YOUR TENANT'S deployment name, not a fixed id — Azure gateways
# rename/retire model families over time. Run `npm run azure:doctor --
# --target gpt --fix` to discover and lock in the real one; gpt-5.6-terra
# below is just an illustrative example.
# AZURE_OPENAI_GPT_DEPLOYMENT=gpt-5.6-terra

# Final reviewer = Opus (replaces Gemini). Defaults to claude-opus-4-7
# (100K TPM / 100 RPM — large enough to hold a full audit transcript in one call;
# the older claude-opus-4-6 at 10K TPM can 429 unrecoverably on big audits).
# AZURE_FOUNDRY_CLAUDE_DEPLOYMENT=claude-opus-4-7

# Arch-index summariser = Sonnet on Foundry. Defaults to claude-sonnet-4-6.
# AZURE_FOUNDRY_SUMMARY_DEPLOYMENT=claude-sonnet-4-6

# Embedding deployment (defaults to text-embedding-3-large if unset). This is a
# DEPLOYMENT name, and it is baked into the request path — set it to whatever your
# resource actually calls the deployment, which need not match the model id.
# `npm run azure:doctor -- --fix` probes and locks in the real name.
# AZURE_OPENAI_EMBED_DEPLOYMENT=text-embedding-3-large

# How Foundry serves Claude. Default `anthropic` is correct for Azure AI Foundry
# (native Anthropic Messages at `/anthropic/v1/messages` — verified live). The
# auth header comes from AZURE_CLAUDE_ROUTE, not from here. `openai` is for the
# rare OpenAI-shaped Foundry deployment.
# AZURE_CLAUDE_API_SHAPE=anthropic

# ── Rate-limit management ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Check your live limits any time:  npm run azure:limits
# Global cap on simultaneous in-flight Azure LLM calls (paces bursts so they
# don't blow the requests-per-minute limit). Default 4 — tuned for 100K-TPM/
# 100-RPM workhorse deployments (TPM-bound on the large GPT passes). Raise toward
# 6–8 if RPM-bound batch work (embeddings at 600 RPM, summaries at 200) dominates.
# AZURE_MAX_CONCURRENCY=4
# SDK retries on 429 honour Azure's Retry-After / reset headers. Default 6.
# AZURE_MAX_RETRIES=6

# ── Logical model sentinels (KEEP as sentinels — NOT deployment names) ─────────
# These stay on the normal sentinel contract for logging/pricing. The Azure
# wire-level model comes from the AZURE_*_DEPLOYMENT vars above. Leaving these
# commented uses the defaults (latest-gpt / latest-opus).
# OPENAI_AUDIT_MODEL=latest-gpt
# CLAUDE_FINAL_REVIEW_MODEL=latest-opus

# The live model catalog 404s against Azure-only access, so it's auto-skipped
# when the Azure profile is active. Set explicitly only to be belt-and-braces.
# MODEL_CATALOG_REFRESH=skip

# ── Postgres store (local for now; AWS/Azure-managed is just a different DSN) ──
# Canonical names. The legacy AUDIT_POSTGRES_URL / AUDIT_POSTGRES_SSL_MODE
# aliases still work (with a one-time deprecation notice).
AUDIT_DB_URL=postgres://postgres:<password>@localhost:5432/audit_loop
AUDIT_DB_SSL_MODE=disable
# AUDIT_STORE=postgres   # optional; if set, a missing DSN fails fast (no silent local-only).

# ── Provider-agnostic final review (OpenAI-compatible gateways) ───────────────
# The final-review gate isn't Gemini-only. Point it at ANY OpenAI-shaped endpoint
# (OpenRouter / Together / Fireworks / Groq / vLLM / Ollama / LM Studio) — useful
# when a corporate profile runs its own model for the reviewer role. These routes
# are EXPLICIT-SELECTION-ONLY (never auto-detect), so a stray shared OPENROUTER_API_KEY
# can't silently route code egress to a third party.
#   node scripts/gemini-review.mjs set-provider openai-compatible   # or: openrouter
# FINAL_REVIEW_BASE_URL=https://your-gateway/v1     # openai-compatible (overrides the openrouter preset)
# FINAL_REVIEW_API_KEY=...                          # gateway key (openrouter falls back to OPENROUTER_API_KEY)
# FINAL_REVIEW_MODEL=your/model-id                  # concrete gateway id, passed verbatim (no sentinel remap)
# Background-safe termination watchdog (guarantees the CLI exits even if a provider
# wedges). Default 600000; clamped [60000, 3600000]; floored at 2×GEMINI_REVIEW_TIMEOUT_MS+60000.
# FINAL_REVIEW_HARD_DEADLINE_MS=600000
